  <title>5.14.4: Using Function Notation to Compare Linear and Exponential Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01494</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.14.4: Using Function Notation to Compare Linear and Exponential Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ec7b5309-a035-4c88-8850-0d4ee2e9a770</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>The function \(f\) is given by \(f(x)=10x+3\), and the function \(g\) is given by \(g(x)=2^x\). For each question, show your reasoning.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Which function reaches 50 first? 
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f(5)=53\) and \(g(5)=32\), so \(f\) reaches 50 first. 
    
  </item>
  <item> Which function reaches 100 first? 
    
    <emphasis>Answer</emphasis><emphasis>:</emphasis>

    \(f(7)=73\) and \(g(7)=128\), so \(g\) reaches 100 first.
  </item>
</list>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>Points to emphasize</title>
<para>Take a few minutes in the next lesson to discuss a few approaches to estimate values to compare the two functions.</para></section>
</content>
